{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggZryJf9Hmk8dNKo273b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For a tetrahedral d⁷ ion I used the octahedral d³ Tanabe–Sugano relations (the hole formalism). This gives **Dq ≈ 433 cm⁻¹** and **B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹**. These values reproduce all three bands to within about 3 cm⁻¹. C cannot be found from spin-allowed bands.\n\n**Band assignments**\n\nThe ground term is ⁴A₂(F). The three spin-allowed transitions are:\n- ν₁ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂(F) at 4 330 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F) at 7 520 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P) at 18 640 cm⁻¹\n\n**Energy expressions**\n\n- ν₁ = 10Dq\n- ν₂ and ν₃ = 7.5B + 15Dq ∓ ½·√(225B² − 180·Dq·B + 100Dq²)\n- Adding the last two gives ν₂ + ν₃ = 15B + 30Dq.\n\n**Solving**\n\n- From ν₁: 10Dq = 4 330, so **Dq = 433 cm⁻¹** (Δt ≈ 4 330 cm⁻¹).\n- From the sum: ν₂ + ν₃ = 26 160 = 15B + 12 990, so 15B = 13 170 and **B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Check**\n\n- 7.5B + 15Dq = 6 585 + 6 495 = 13 080 cm⁻¹\n- The square-root term is √(1.2377 × 10⁸) ≈ 11 125, so half of it is 5 563 cm⁻¹.\n- ν₂ = 13 080 − 5 563 ≈ 7 517 cm⁻¹ (observed 7 520).\n- ν₃ = 13 080 + 5 563 ≈ 18 643 cm⁻¹ (observed 18 640).\n\nThe nephelauxetic ratio is β = B(complex)/B(free ion) ≈ 878/1 120 ≈ 0.78. This is a typical value for a tetrahedral Co(II) complex.\n\n**Why C is not given:** all three bands are quartet → quartet transitions. Their energies depend only on Dq and B, so the data contain no information about C. Finding C would need a spin-forbidden band, for example a transition to a doublet state.\n\n{\"Dq\": 433, \"B\": 878, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fit of three spin-allowed bands with d3-type (Td d7) expressions gives Dq and B; C is undetermined from spin-allowed bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":168,"cache_read_input_tokens":627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens"